{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-34624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(-1,-\\pi )$ where $t=\\pi $, given that $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖=\\pi ,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-34624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(\\pi )=⟨-1,-\\pi ⟩$,  since the point $P(-1,-\\pi )$ is at the tip of $r(\\pi ).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(\\pi )$ and $‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{\\pi }{1}=\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=\\pi $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\pi ) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\pi )}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=⟨0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi )+R(\\pi )N(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,-\\pi ⟩+\\pi ⟨0,1⟩=⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-34624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30bcf9071be7d18124dfee73c20dc0aafdfa3708ba4ca94786ae5b3634a8e54d"} {"question" "q-34624" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-34624" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The center is $(-1,-2\\pi )$ and the radius is $1$"] ["text" "The center is $(-1,-2\\pi )$ and the radius is $\\pi $"] ["text" "The center is $(-1,-\\pi )$ and the radius is $\\pi $"] ["text" "The center is $(-1,0)$ and the radius is $1$"] ["text" "The center is $(-1,0)$ and the radius is $\\pi $"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-34624" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The center is $(-1,0)$ and the radius is $\\pi $"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-98665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{cos}^{3}⁡t,{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-98665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-3{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-3{cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-3{sin}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}){(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{3},6(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}){(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩\\times (\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})}^{2}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\,-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot 2\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}=\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}}{\\,\\frac{9}{4}\\,}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-98665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30bcf9071be7d18124dfee73c20dc0aafdfa3708ba4ca94786ae5b3634a8e54d"} {"question" "q-98665" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-98665" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-98665" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-98708" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=4$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨4\\sqrt{t-3},t-1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-98708" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(4)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(4)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t-3}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t-3}},1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(4) & =⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(4)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t-3}}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(t-3)^{3}}},0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(4) & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(4)$ and ${r}^{″}(4)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4) & =⟨2,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(4) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(4){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4)‖}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^{3}}{\\,1\\,}=5\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-98708" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30bcf9071be7d18124dfee73c20dc0aafdfa3708ba4ca94786ae5b3634a8e54d"} {"question" "q-98708" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-98708" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "5\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "5\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-98708" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5\\sqrt{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-132122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-132122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨1,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-{sec}^{2}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨0,-{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-132122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30bcf9071be7d18124dfee73c20dc0aafdfa3708ba4ca94786ae5b3634a8e54d"} {"question" "q-132122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-132122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-132122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-132208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(3,1)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=2,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-132208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨3,1⟩,$ since the point $P(3,1)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨3,1⟩+2⟨-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,1⟩+⟨-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-132208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30bcf9071be7d18124dfee73c20dc0aafdfa3708ba4ca94786ae5b3634a8e54d"} {"question" "q-132208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082477/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ba5d7de45708ad48243b960a114b542c63314c92086741f9cee5430eddba015"} {"question" "q-132208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The center is $⟨-1,1⟩$ and the radius is $2$"] ["text" "The center is $⟨1,1⟩$ and the radius is $2$"] ["text" "The center is $⟨2,1⟩$ and the radius is $1$"] ["text" "The center is $⟨3,2⟩$ and the radius is $1$"] ["text" "The center is $⟨3,3⟩$ and the radius is $2$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Cou